 system we are talking about, just different ways to log in to a terminal. I tried copying the entire PATH from a ssh connection to my X2go terminal (noticed the paths were not identical), but that did resolve the issue. There must be some environment variable or setting which makes the exifread/mutagen search much more ineffective. I tried to start a new X2go session as well but that did not change anything.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1580095232,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3ODU3ODgzNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4085#issuecomment-578578836"},"message":"ok, never used x2go... seems quite a powerful abomination (old me got used to vnc with simple/client server -- here there is bunch of packages, not even clear what to install on server... I will learn ;-) ). So - installed and reproduced: \n\n- not knowing principles of its work, it seems it passes some X settings from client into remote server. on laptop (client) I have high res display (3840x2160) with large fonts set for UI elements.  Remote mate desktop seems has inherited them (yikes)\n- for me the \"waiting\" happened at different point in external_versions checking, immediately suggesting `keyring` (and/or used by it secretstorage) to \"blame\" for trying to reach out to the local (client)\n```\n$\u003e DATALAD_LOG_TIMESTAMP=1 datalad -l1 wtf\n...\n2020-01-26 21:52:23,319 [Level 8] Finished running ['7z'] with status 0 \nGkr-Message: 21:52:48.436: secret service operation failed: Did not receive a reply. Possible causes include: the remote application did not send a reply, the message bus security policy blocked the reply, the reply timeout expired, or the network connection was broken.\n2020-01-26 21:53:13,483 [DEBUG  ] Module patool seems to be not present \n...\n```\n(so there is almost a minute distance between two log lines).\n- I have added, and directly pushed 5658084604a02a46779c4a7ed610a302c9e4557f, which prints now the log line for what module is asked for, which confirms:\n  ```\n2020-01-26 22:00:12,068 [Level 5] Requested to provide version for keyring \nGkr-Message: 22:01:02.536: secret service operation failed: Did not receive a reply. Possible causes include: the remote application did not send a reply, the message bus security policy blocked the reply, the reply timeout expired, or the network connection was broken.\n2020-01-26 22:01:02,538 [Level 5] Requested to provide version for keyrings.alt \n```\n- and doing `python -c \"import keyring\"` in terminal results also in that long waiting time.\n\n- So it is something to troubleshoot in keyring/x2go interaction.  There is an ongoing effort (https://github.com/jaraco/keyring/issues/403#issuecomment-540165970) to improve overall import time by not loading all backends, or even to no load them until actually keyring is used (would be the best resolution). Just to not hide our \"finding\", since not reported against keyring yet, filed https://github.com/jaraco/keyring/issues/420 .\nI am not sure if we should (could) do anything here on datalad side ATM.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1580095232,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDM0MzkyNTUxOQ=="},"target":"f56225cd1ce1ec2d87f8e23d957d9d1bbf39ba51925cb5817b462c7b460ba121","message":"ok, never used x2go... seems quite a powerful abomination (old me got used to vnc with simple/client server -- here there is bunch of packages, not even clear what to install on server... I will learn ;-) ). So - installed and reproduced: \n\n- not knowing principles of its work, it seems it passes some X settings from client into remote server. on laptop (client) I have high res display (3840x2160) with large fonts set for UI elements.  Remote mate desktop seems has inherited them (yikes)\n- for me the \"waiting\" happened at different point in external_versions checking, immediately suggesting `keyring` (and/or used by it secretstorage) to \"blame\" for trying to reach out to the local (client)\n```\n$\u003e DATALAD_LOG_TIMESTAMP=1 datalad -l1 wtf\n...\n2020-01-26 21:52:23,319 [Level 8] Finished running ['7z'] with status 0 \nGkr-Message: 21:52:48.436: secret service operation failed: Did not receive a reply. Possible causes include: the remote application did not send a reply, the message bus security policy blocked the reply, the reply timeout expired, or the network connection was broken.\n2020-01-26 21:53:13,483 [DEBUG  ] Module patool seems to be not present \n...\n```\n(so there is almost a minute distance between two log lines).\n- I have added, and directly pushed 5658084604a02a46779c4a7ed610a302c9e4557f, which prints now the log line for what module is asked for, which confirms:\n```\n2020-01-26 22:00:12,068 [Level 5] Requested to provide version for keyring \nGkr-Message: 22:01:02.536: secret service operation failed: Did not receive a reply. Possible causes include: the remote application did not send a reply, the message bus security policy blocked the reply, the reply timeout expired, or the network connection was broken.\n2020-01-26 22:01:02,538 [Level 5] Requested to provide version for keyrings.alt \n```\n\n- and doing `python -c \"import keyring\"` in terminal results also in that long waiting time.\n\n- So it is something to troubleshoot in keyring/x2go interaction.  There is an ongoing effort (https://github.com/jaraco/keyring/issues/403#issuecomment-540165970) to improve overall import time by not loading all backends, or even to no load them until actually keyring is used (would be the best resolution). Just to not hide our \"finding\", since not reported against keyring yet, filed https://github.com/jaraco/keyring/issues/420 .\nI am not sure if we should (could) do anything here on datalad side ATM.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1580106400,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5ODA2MjYwMTM="},"added":["external-tools"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1580106400,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5ODA2MjYwMTU="},"added":["severity-minor"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1580106400,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5ODA2MjYwMTY="},"added":["UX"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1580106554,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3ODYwODE5OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4085#issuecomment-578608199"},"message":"I concur with @yarikoptic \n\nI don't see what we could do. The purpose of `wtf` is to figure out what the environment is, and this latency is a reflection of that environment. If this delay translates to other functionality that doesn't actually require keyring access, I'd consider that a bug to be addressed. The issue at hand, though, is more an annoyance than a bug to me.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1580158256,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3ODk0NjUyMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4085#issuecomment-578946520"},"message":"Waw, @yarikoptic you went to great lengths to reproduce this. I agree that this is a strange issue with no replication. Just worth having here if someone else encounters it. My understanding of backends is minimal, I thought a terminal is an (almost) identical environment whether opened with SSH, Gnome, or XFCE, but go figure.\n\nI am closing this issue now given the special context it requires to be reproduced. Anyone can reopen again if deemed useful.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1580158257,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50Mjk4MzM1NTI5MQ=="},"sta